    deeperDive: [
      { q: 'What is saṃgati (association) and why does it matter?', a: 'Saṃgati means "togetherness," "association," or "meeting." It\'s a stronger concept than mere simultaneity — it implies an intimate, necessary connection. Desire and the desirous one aren\'t just accidentally co-present; they\'re LINKED. The question is: what kind of "linking" can ground this association? Neither identity (no linking needed) nor inherent difference (no linking possible) works.', realLifeExample: 'Dance partners are "associated" — they move together intentionally. Two strangers on a street are "simultaneous" but not "associated." The desire-desirous connection is like dance partners, not like strangers.' },
      { q: 'Why does identity make association redundant?', a: 'If A and B are identical (they\'re literally one thing), there\'s nothing for A to be "associated with." Association requires TWO things in relationship. One thing doesn\'t have a relationship with itself — it just IS. So identity eliminates the possibility of association, which eliminates the kind of togetherness desire and the desirous one exhibit.', realLifeExample: 'You don\'t "associate" with yourself at a party. You might talk to yourself, but that\'s not a social relationship. Association requires TWO distinct beings.' },
      { q: 'Why does inherent difference make association arbitrary?', a: 'If A and B are inherently different (they have completely independent natures), there\'s no REASON for them to be associated. Any association would be accidental. But desire and the desirous one are NECESSARILY associated — you can\'t have one without the other. This necessity can\'t arise from independence.', realLifeExample: 'Two strangers might end up at the same restaurant by coincidence (arbitrary). But a violinist and their violin are necessarily associated (one implies the other). Inherent difference only gives you the first model; desire/desirous need the second.' },
      { q: 'How does entanglement provide "non-classical association"?', a: 'Classically, particles are either the same particle (identity — no association) or independent particles (separable — arbitrary association). Entanglement creates a third category: particles that are DISTINCT (not identical) yet NECESSARILY correlated (not independent). This is a genuinely new kind of togetherness that classical physics can\'t explain. It\'s the quantum analog of dependent arising\'s middle way.', realLifeExample: 'Before quantum mechanics, physicists only had two models: same thing or separate things. Entanglement was like discovering that TWO things could be intimately connected without being the same — a new category of relationship.' },
      { q: 'Is this the same argument as v.4?', a: 'Related but deeper. V.4 showed identity → no simultaneity, difference → problematic simultaneity. V.5 shows identity → no ASSOCIATION, difference → no ASSOCIATION. Association is a stronger requirement than mere simultaneity — it requires intimate, necessary connection. V.5 raises the bar: not just "can they co-occur?" but "can they be genuinely LINKED?"', realLifeExample: 'V.4: "Can these two people be in the same room?" V.5: "Can these two people be dance PARTNERS?" Being in the same room (simultaneity) is easier than being partners (association). V.5 shows even the stronger relationship fails under inherent existence.' },
      { q: 'What is the philosophical significance of "without association"?', a: '"Without association" (vinā saṃgatena) means the togetherness would be empty of genuine connection. Simultaneity without association is like two strangers on a bus — they\'re together in space and time, but there\'s no real connection. Nāgārjuna argues that under inherent existence, desire and the desirous one would be like strangers on a bus — together but unconnected. This contradicts our experience of their intimate linkage.', realLifeExample: 'Two apps running simultaneously on your phone aren\'t "associated" — they\'re just co-present. But your fingers and your hands ARE associated — they\'re functionally linked. Desire and the desirous one are like fingers and hands, not like apps.' },
    ],
